<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Dinant</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Dinant is a town on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> in the province of Namur, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it belonged to the Prince-Bishopric of Liège and was famous for its brass and copperware, known as dinanderie. It was sacked by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> in 1466, and in August 1914 German troops killed some 670 of its civilians.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/dinant.jpg" alt="Dinant" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: jiuguangw (Flickr) · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dinant_reflected.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Dinant lies in the Upper Meuse valley, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ardennes">Ardennes</a>, where the river cuts deeply into the western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/condroz">Condroz</a> plateau. Between the rock face and the river the original settlement had little room, and the town grew into a long, thin line along the river shore. The navigable river carried the raw materials of its brassware to the workshops and the finished goods away, and the limestone cliffs above supported a quarrying industry producing black marble and bluestone. Its name comes from the Celtic Divo-Nanto, meaning Sacred Valley.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The area was populated in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>, Celtic and Roman times. Dinant is first mentioned as a settlement in the 7th century, when Perpète of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maastricht">Maastricht</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishop</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tongeren">Tongeren</a>, moved his main residence there and founded the church of Saint Vincent. In the 11th century the emperor Henry IV granted the Prince-Bishopric of Liège market and justice rights over the town, which became one of its 23 principal cities, the bonnes villes. The first stone bridge on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> dates from the end of that century.</p><p>In 1466, during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/liege-wars">Liège Wars</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-the-good">Philip the Good</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundy">Burgundy</a>, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> punished an uprising by casting 800 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burghers">burghers</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a> and setting fire to the city. Late medieval Dinant and its rival Bouvignes specialised in dinanderie, objects cast and finished in a silvery brass alloy, among them aquamaniles, candlesticks and altar furniture, supplied through the Meuse valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rhineland">Rhineland</a>. After the Bishopric of Liège was ceded to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in 1795, the dinanderies fell out of fashion and the economy rested on leather tanning and playing cards.</p><p>On 15 August 1914 French and German troops fought for the town in the Battle of Dinant, and on 23 August Saxon troops of the German Army summarily executed 674 inhabitants, the largest massacre committed by the Germans in 1914. In 1940 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/erwin-rommel">Erwin Rommel</a>'s 7th Panzer Division took the town on 13 May after crossing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meuse">Meuse</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Dinant is a town of the province of Namur, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wallonia">Wallonia</a>, about 30 km south of the city of Namur. Its landmark is the Collegiate Church of Notre Dame, rebuilt in Gothic style after falling rock from the cliff partly destroyed the Romanesque church in 1227, with an onion-domed tower. Above it rises the Citadel, first built in the 11th century and rebuilt in 1821.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Dinant” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The German advance was marked by massacres of civilians at <b>Dinant</b> and Leuven, where the university library was burned.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-belgium">Kingdom of Belgium · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Burgundian pressure grew under Philip the Good, whose forces sacked the copper-working town of <b>Dinant</b> in 1466.</blockquote><a href="/empire/prince-bishopric-of-liege">Prince-Bishopric of Liège · Military</a></li></ul></section>
